{"type":"message","id":"701e0771","parentId":"4c4d79fb","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:55:44.864Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have:\n- gateway-wrapper.bat\n- install-gateway-service.ps1\n- SERVICE_SETUP.md (documentation)\n- adjust-power-settings.ps1\n\nNext, I need to verify that the openclaw gateway command works correctly. But I can't reliably run commands due to the exec tool limitations. However, I can still check if the built gateway exists (dist/entry.js or dist/entry.mjs). Because the openclaw.mjs script imports from dist/entry.js. So the project must be built first. I should note that in the documentation.\n\nI can also try to check if the built files exist using the read tool to see if dist/entry.js exists. That will tell us if the project is already built.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check if the project has been built (dist/entry.js exists):"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ca8a979145f449eb9f22e963","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\dist\\entry.js","limit":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":31224,"output":392,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31616,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771419340030}}
{"type":"message","id":"dd32c680","parentId":"701e0771","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:55:44.886Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ca8a979145f449eb9f22e963","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env node\nimport { createRequire } from \"node:module\";\nimport { spawn } from \"node:child_process\";\nimport process$1 from \"node:process\";\nimport os from \"node:os\";\nimport path from \"node:path\";\nimport fs from \"node:fs\";\nimport chalk, { Chalk } from \"chalk\";\nimport { Logger } from \"tslog\";\nimport JSON5 from \"json5\";\n\n[2448 more lines in file. Use offset=11 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1771419344883}}
{"type":"message","id":"8a17192d","parentId":"dd32c680","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:55:52.110Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Great! The dist/entry.js exists. That means the project is built and the openclaw.mjs should work. Good.\n\nNow I should create a comprehensive service setup that is robust. One thing to consider: The gateway might need to run with a specific working directory for config files (~/.openclaw). Usually, the gateway looks for config in the user's home directory. That should be fine.\n\nBut also, the gateway might need to run with the correct Node.js environment. Since the wrapper uses `node`, it should find node in PATH. But as a service, the PATH environment may be different. We can configure NSSM to include the full path to node.exe if needed.\n\nI'll update the wrapper to use full path to node if possible, or we can set the environment in NSSM.\n\nLet me check where Node.js is typically installed on this system. Usually Node is installed in Program Files or under the user's AppData. But I can't easily find it without exec. I'll assume Node is in PATH for the user, and for the service we might need to set the PATH environment variable explicitly.\n\nIn the install script, I could optionally detect Node path and set it. But that's complicated. I'll add a note that if the service fails to start because `node` is not recognized, adjust the wrapper to use the full path to node.exe, or set the AppEnvironment in NSSM to include the Node.js bin directory in PATH.\n\nI'll also add a note that the gateway may need network access, firewall rules, etc.\n\nNow, I need to test: put laptop to sleep, wake, verify gateway is running and WhatsApp reconnects. But I can't simulate sleep programmatically in this environment easily. I'll provide manual test steps.\n\nAlso, note: The gateway's \"gateway run\" command may block and run until interrupted. That's fine for a service.\n\nNow, I need to consider something: The task mentions \"Ensure OpenClaw gateway stays running when the laptop goes to sleep and wakes.\" Typically, when a laptop goes to sleep, all processes are suspended. They don't get CPU time. When it wakes, they resume. However, network connections may be dropped. Some services might not handle the resume gracefully and could crash or need to be restarted. That's why using a service with auto-restart is a good approach: even if the gateway crashes on wake, the service will restart it quickly. Also, some applications need special handling to wake from sleep (like adjusting power settings to prevent sleep). But since the user may still want the laptop to sleep to save battery, we should not disable sleep entirely but rather ensure the gateway recovers quickly. That's the NSSM auto-restart.\n\nAdditionally, Windows has a feature called \"Wake on LAN\" or \"Allow wake timers\" which might help. But that's optional.\n\nLet me also think: The gateway might need to be configured to use a specific port. The wrapper uses port 8080. But the gateway default might be something else (like 8080 or 18789). I'll make the port configurable by editing the wrapper.\n\nBetter yet, I could read an environment variable or a config file. But for simplicity, I'll keep it simple.\n\nNow, the task says \"Report back with steps taken and any issues.\" So I'll produce a final report summarizing what I did.\n\nBefore I finalize, I should ensure the wrapper script uses the correct commands. The openclaw.mjs expects subcommands like \"gateway run\". Let's search the source for the gateway command definition. I'll try to locate the CLI commands in the repository.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me search for where the gateway command is defined to ensure correct usage:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_92abcd1d646f4a09ab3a5e55","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\src\\commands"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":