{"type":"message","id":"155ab446","parentId":"0e508934","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:57:02.824Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I've created comprehensive files and documentation. However, there is something else: The task says \"use the gateway-wrapper.bat we made or direct command\". It says \"we made\" implying it already exists. Could it be that there is already a wrapper somewhere? Let me search for any existing gateway-wrapper.bat in the entire D: drive, if possible. But given exec limitations, I can't. However, maybe the intention is that I should use the wrapper that was previously created in this workspace by the main agent or system. Since I don't see it, I created one. That should be fine.\n\nAlso, the task mentions \"likely D:\\Software\\codex\\openclaw.mjs or similar\". So maybe the actual openclaw executable is not in the workspace but in a different location. I should note that in the documentation. In the install script default parameters, I set OpenClawPath to the workspace. But the user may need to change it to D:\\Software\\codex. That's why the script has parameters. I'll add a note in QUICKSTART that the default path points to the workspace and to adjust if using a different install location.\n\nNow, the task also says \"ensure the gateway stays running when the laptop goes to sleep and wakes.\" The solution: run as Windows service with auto-restart. That is done. Additionally, adjusting power settings can prevent sleep entirely, but that's optional.\n\nNow, I need to produce a final report to the main agent. According to the subagent instructions: \"When complete, your final response should include:\n- What you accomplished or found\n- Any relevant details the main agent should know\n- Keep it concise but informative\"\n\nSo I need to output a concise summary, not the entire documentation. I'll send a final message summarizing what I did, the files created, and any important notes. The detailed documentation is in the files. I should also mention any issues encountered.\n\nIssues encountered:\n- The exec tool was not returning output, making it difficult to verify system state. But I worked around by using read on specific files and by creating the necessary files based on assumptions.\n- I couldn't actually test the service installation or sleep/wake cycle due to environment limitations. But I provided instructions.\n\nI should also note that NSSM must be installed separately; I didn't include it because it's external.\n\nNow, before finalizing, let me double-check the wrapper: It uses `node` command. That's fine if node is in PATH. For a service, the PATH might be different. NSSM by default inherits the system environment. The Node.js installer usually adds to the system PATH. If not, we can set the full path. I've added a note in the troubleshooting.\n\nAlso, the wrapper includes `--force`. This is to avoid interactive prompts. Good.\n\nNow, let me also consider that the gateway might need to be started with `--config` or `--data-dir` if the config is not in the default location. The default config is likely in `~/.openclaw/config.json`. The service runs under LocalSystem account, whose home is `C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\systemprofile`. That means the gateway would look for config in `C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\systemprofile\\.openclaw\\config.json` if using the default. That's not the user's config! That's a critical issue!\n\nIf the service runs as LocalSystem (the default for Windows services), the HOME environment variable points to the system profile, not the user's profile. So the gateway would not find the user's OpenClaw configuration (credentials, channel settings). That would cause the gateway to start with default empty config, not having WhatsApp credentials, etc. That's a major problem.\n\nWe need to run the service under a specific user account (the user who owns the OpenClaw config) or set the HOME environment variable, or use a config file path override.\n\nThe AGENTS.md says: \"Web provider stores creds at ~/.openclaw/credentials/\". So they use the home directory. For the service to use the existing credentials, it must run as that user. Typically, we'd create a dedicated user account (e.g., openclaw) and install the service to run under that account. Or run under the current user account but configure it to store credentials there.\n\nIn the install script, we should add an option to specify the user account. By default, NSSM runs with the same privileges as the user who installed the service (if not changed). But if we run the install script as Administrator, the service will be installed but will run as LocalSystem by default unless we change it. Actually, NSSM by default runs the service as LocalSystem. So that would break.\n\nWe need to modify the install script to:\n- Either run the service as the current user (the one installing) or as a specified user.\n- Or set the HOME environment variable to point to the correct user profile.\n- Or use the `--config` flag to specify config path.\n\nLet's check the OpenClaw CLI options. I could try to find if there's a `--config` or `--data-dir` flag. I recall from the docs maybe there's `--config` or environment variable. But I'm not sure. Searching my memory: OpenClaw uses `~/.openclaw` for config and data. There may be a `--config` option to specify a config file. But I'm not certain.\n\nBetter: Run the service under the user account. That's the simplest: the user's profile already has the config. In the install script, after installing the service, we can change the service's logon account to the current user or a specified user.\n\nIn PowerShell, we can use `Set-Service` with -Credential, but that doesn't change the service account; that's for the service's logon. Actually, to change the service's credentials, we use WMI or sc.exe. But NSSM provides `nssm set <service> ObjectName \"domain\\user\" \"password\"` to set the account.\n\nSo in the install script, I should add a prompt or parameter to specify the user account. Or automatically use the current user.\n\nBut note: If we run the install script as Administrator, the current user might be the admin account which has the config. That could work. But then the service would run with admin privileges which is okay but not necessary.\n\nLet's add a parameter to the install script: -RunAsUser and -RunAsPassword. Or we can set it interactively. For now, I'll add a step to set the service to run as the current user if a parameter is provided. But to keep it simple, I'll add a note that the service runs as LocalSystem by default and the user may need to adjust the service's \"Log On\" tab to run as their user account, or use NSSM to set the ObjectName.\n\nActually, I should update the install script to automatically configure the service to run as the current user (the one executing the script) to preserve the user's profile environment. But that would require getting the current user's credentials. PowerShell can get the current user's name, but the password would need to be supplied for the service to log on as that user automatically. Windows services can't store passwords in plaintext easily; we can use `sc.exe config` but it prompts for password. There's also `Set-Service -Credential` which requires a PSCredential object with password. That would require the user to input password. That's not ideal for unattended install.\n\nAnother approach: Keep the service as LocalSystem but set the HOME environment variable to point to the user's profile. We can set NSSM's AppEnvironment to include `HOME=C:\\Users\\YourUserName`. That might work because many apps respect HOME. However, OpenClaw may use `os.homedir()` from Node.js which uses Windows API GetEnvironmentVariable('HOME') or USERPROFILE. So setting HOME might be enough. But better to also set USERPROFILE? Node.js's `os.homedir()` returns the home directory based on environment variables: On Windows, it checks `HOME`, `USERPROFILE`, `HOMEDRIVE`+`HOMEPATH`. So we can set USERPROFILE.\n\nBut the user's profile path is typically `C:\\Users\\Username`. We can get it via `$env:USERPROFILE` from the current user. In the install script run as that user, we could set the service's environment to include USERPROFILE. However, if the service runs as LocalSystem, setting USERPROFILE to a user's profile might be a security risk, but it's fine for this scenario. So we could do:\n\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"USERPROFILE=C:\\Users\\YourName\"\n\nBut we need to determine the correct user. We could default to the user who runs the script, but then the service would still run as LocalSystem but with environment pointing to that user's profile. That might work because the gateway will read config from that path.\n\nLet's implement that: In the install script, after installing, we set the environment variable HOME or USERPROFILE to the current user's profile. That way the gateway will find the config. But careful: If the script is run as Administrator, the current user's profile is the admin's profile, which might not have the config if config is in a standard user account. That's okay because we assume the admin is the one who installed OpenClaw.\n\nSimplify: In the documentation, instruct to run the install script as the same user that runs OpenClaw normally (i.e., the user whose config you want to use). That user should be the one who has the `.openclaw` folder. And we should not change the service account; keep it as LocalSystem but set environment variables to that user's profile. That ensures the config is found without needing to run the service under that user account (which would require password). However, there's a nuance: The service runs as LocalSystem, which has its own profile. We can tell the gateway to use a specific config directory via environment variable or command-line flag. I need to check if OpenClaw supports a custom home directory via env var. Node.js's `os.homedir()` uses environment. So if we set `HOME` or `USERPROFILE` in the service environment, it should work.\n\nThus, I'll modify the wrapper to not set its own working directory only, but also the configuration will be pulled from the environment set by NSSM.\n\nIn the install script, I'll add:\n\n```powershell\n# Set user profile environment to current user's profile for config location\n$userProfile = [Environment]::GetFolderPath('UserProfile')\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"USERPROFILE=$userProfile\"\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"HOME=$userProfile\"\n```\n\nBut careful: NSSM's AppEnvironment can be set multiple times, each call appends? Actually, `nssm set <service> AppEnvironment <NAME>=<VALUE>` sets one environment variable. To set multiple, you call it multiple times. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we might need to set the PATH to include Node if not in system PATH. But that's less likely.\n\nNow, is there any risk of multiple environment variables interfering? Should be fine.\n\nI'll update the install script accordingly.\n\nBut first, let me consider: The wrapper sets working directory to OpenClawPath. That's fine. But the config location is based on home directory. If we set USERPROFILE to the real user's profile, it will look in that user's `.openclaw`. That's what we want.\n\nNow, there is a nuance: If the OpenClaw config is not in the default location (maybe user set `OPENCLAW_CONFIG` env var), we need to preserve that. But we don't know. We could also copy the config to the service's context, but that's messy. Simpler: run the service as the actual user. But that would require storing password. I think setting environment variables is acceptable.\n\nNow, should we also set APPDATA, LOCALAPPDATA, etc.? Probably not needed.\n\nLet's implement the environment setting in the install script after the working directory line, and also after the logging configuration, but before starting service.\n\nI'll update the script.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"I need to address a critical issue: the service runs under LocalSystem by default, which uses a different home directory. The OpenClaw gateway uses `~/.openclaw` for config and credentials. So we must ensure the service uses the correct user profile. I'll modify the install script to set environment variables pointing to the installing user's profile:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b0505339796048ff854a8012","name":"edit","arguments":{"file_path":"D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw\\install-gateway-service.ps1","new_text":"# Optional: Set working directory (helps with relative paths)\nWrite-Host \"Setting working directory to $OpenClawPath...\"\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppDirectory \"$OpenClawPath\"\n\n# IMPORTANT: Configure user profile environment so gateway finds ~/.openclaw config\n# The service runs as LocalSystem by default. We set USERPROFILE/HOME to the current user's profile\n$currentUserProfile = [Environment]::GetFolderPath('UserProfile')\nWrite-Host \"Setting USERPROFILE and HOME to '$currentUserProfile' for config location...\"\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"USERPROFILE=$currentUserProfile\"\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"HOME=$currentUserProfile\"\n\n# Also add Node.js to PATH if not in system PATH (optional, uncomment if needed)\n# $nodePath = \"C:\\Program Files\\nodejs\"\n# & $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"PATH=$nodePath;%PATH%\"\n\n# Optionally, set NODE_ENV=production\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"NODE_ENV=production\"","old_text":"# Optional: Set working directory (helps with relative paths)\nWrite-Host \"Setting working directory to $OpenClawPath...\"\n& $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppDirectory \"$OpenClawPath\"\n\n# Optional: Set environment variables if needed\n# & $NssmPath set $ServiceName AppEnvironment \"NODE_ENV=production\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":37377,"output":6071,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":43448,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771419402509}}
