{"type":"message","id":"25c7dad5","parentId":"3a68023e","timestamp":"2026-02-18T12:57:35.537Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now the service will have environment variables pointing to the user's profile. That should make the gateway find the config. However, there's a nuance: The service runs as LocalSystem, but we're setting its environment to mimic a user. That's okay. The gateway will read/write config in that user's profile. That user should be the one who originally set up OpenClaw and has credentials. If the install script is run as a different user, it will set that user's profile. So the operator must run the installer as the user whose config they want to use. That's acceptable.\n\nNow, I should also consider that the wrapper sets OPENCLAW_PATH and node. That's fine.\n\nNow, what about the power settings? I created a script. Good.\n\nNow, one more thing: The task says \"Ensure OpenClaw gateway stays running when the laptop goes to sleep and wakes.\" But the service might itself be stopped by Windows during sleep if the service is set to \"Manual\" or if the system enters hibernation. Actually, Windows services continue running when the system goes to sleep? No, during sleep the entire system state is suspended; all services are paused. When the system wakes, services resume automatically. However, if the system goes into hibernation (S4), the system state is saved to disk and then powered off; upon power on, services start fresh. In that case, the service should automatically start because it's set to Automatic. So after wake from hibernation, the service will start. That's covered by automatic startup. But if the machine just goes to sleep (S3), all processes are frozen and resumed. That's not a restart; the gateway will continue from where it left off, but network connections may be broken. The gateway should handle that. If it crashes during resume, NSSM will restart it. So the plan is solid.\n\nNow, I need to update the documentation to reflect the environment variable changes. I'll add a note in QUICKSTART about the USERPROFILE/HOME being set automatically. Also note that the service runs as LocalSystem but uses the installer's user profile.\n\nNow, also note that the wrapper's OPENCLAW_PATH should point to the installation location. In the workspace it's the dev build; in production it might be elsewhere.\n\nNow, let me also create a simple script to check if the gateway is actually listening on the port and if the service is healthy. But that's extra.\n\nNow, final step: As a subagent, I need to report back to the main agent with a concise summary. I'll produce a final message that includes:\n\n- Files created: gateway-wrapper.bat, install-gateway-service.ps1, adjust-power-settings.ps1, SERVICE_SETUP.md, QUICKSTART.md.\n- Key features: Windows service with NSSM, auto-restart, logging, environment config for user profile.\n- Important notes: NSSM must be installed, run installer as the correct user, adjust paths if needed, test sleep/wake manually.\n- Issues: The exec tool was not returning output, so I couldn't verify system state or run commands; but the solution is ready for manual execution.\n\nNow, should I also attempt to actually install the service? I could run the install script using exec, but given the exec issues, it might be risky. Better to let the user run it. The task might be expecting me to perform the installation, but it says \"Report back with steps taken and any issues.\" It doesn't explicitly say \"install it\". It says \"Ensure OpenClaw gateway stays running... Actions: 1)... 2) Create/verify a Windows Service... 3) Configure... 4) Optionally adjust... 5) Test...\". It implies I need to do these actions. But can I do them within this subagent? Possibly I can run PowerShell commands to install NSSM service. However, I would need elevated privileges (run as administrator). The subagent may not have those. The system says \"Runtime: admin (running with elevated privileges)\". Actually, looking at the initial system message: \"os=Windows_NT 10.0.26200 (x64) | node=v24.12.0 | model=openrouter... shell=powershell\". It doesn't explicitly say admin. But the task is urgent and might require admin. The subagent might have admin rights. But given the exec tool's weird behavior, I'm hesitant.\n\nBut let's try to run the install script. I can use exec to run PowerShell with the script. However, the script requires admin rights. I could use the `elevated` parameter of exec to run as admin. The exec tool has an `elevated` boolean parameter. Let's try to run the install script with elevated enabled.\n\nI'll try: `exec({command: \"powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\install-gateway-service.ps1\", elevated: true})`.\n\nBut first, I need to test if exec works with elevated. And I need to be sure about the current directory. The script is in D:\\workspace\\appDev\\openclaw. I'll set workdir to that.\n\nGiven the earlier issues where exec didn't show output, maybe it's because the process runs and exits quickly, and I'm not polling correctly. Actually, I saw that when I set background=true, it gave a session ID, but when I polled, it said \"Process exited with code 0. (no output recorded)\". That suggests that the output was empty. But maybe the command didn't output anything. For my install script, it will output a lot. I could start it with background=true and then poll to capture output. But the output might be large. Alternatively, I could run it without background and let it block, and then the exec tool would capture output? But earlier I tried without background and got no output. Hmm.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe I should try to run a simple command with elevated and background to see if output is captured. But I'm concerned about the reliability. Since the task is urgent, I should probably just provide the files and documentation, and instruct the user to run them. The user is likely the main agent's operator. They can run the scripts.\n\nBut the instructions say: \"URGENT: Ensure OpenClaw gateway stays running... Actions: 1) Identify... 2) Create/verify... 3) Configure... 4) Optionally adjust... 5) Test...\" It is a directive to perform these actions, not just provide files. As a subagent, I should attempt to perform them. However, if the environment is not suitable (exec tool issues), I need to report that.\n\nMaybe the exec tool is fine; I just need to wait for the process to finish and then get the output. The default `background` is false, so it should run synchronously and return the output. But I got no output for simple commands. Could it be that the tool's output is sent as a separate message and I'm not seeing it? In the tool response, it just says \"no output\". That might mean the command produced nothing on stdout/stderr, not that there was an error. For PowerShell's Write-Host, it writes to the host, not to stdout? Actually, Write-Host writes to the host UI, which is not captured by standard output redirection. Write-Output writes to the pipeline (stdout). I used Write-Output and still got no output. But maybe the exec tool captures only stdout, and Write-Output writes to stdout. Yet it said no output.\n\nMaybe the tool is not returning output because the process is still running? For a simple command like `Write-Output 'test'`, it should exit immediately. But the exec response said \"Command still running (session ...)\" when I set background=true. Without background, it should block until completion and then return output. But I didn't get any response. Maybe the tool timed out? The default timeout is? Not specified. It could be that the command is hanging. But Write-Output shouldn't hang.\n\nPerhaps I'm using the wrong shell. On Windows, the default shell is PowerShell, but maybe the exec tool is using cmd.exe by default? Or maybe I need to specify pty=true for interactive? Not needed.\n\nGiven the time, I'll assume the exec tool works for starting services. I'll try to install the service now using elevated exec. If it fails, I'll note it.\n\nBut before that, let me check if NSSM exists. I could try to determine if it's installed. I'll try to locate nssm.exe using a command that writes to a file again, but that also had issues. Instead, I could try to directly call `nssm.exe` from a known location. If NSSM is not installed, the script will show an error and I can report that.\n\nLet's try to run the install script with exec, with elevated and background? I'll try without background first and see if it returns output after completion. I'll set a timeout of maybe 30 seconds. I'll also set workdir to the script's directory.\n\nI'll do:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"command\": \"powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\\\install-gateway-service.ps1\",\n  \"workdir\": \"D:\\\\workspace\\\\appDev\\\\openclaw\",\n  \"timeout\": 60,\n  \"elevated\": true\n}\n```\n\nLet me try that.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Given the exec tool's behavior, I'll attempt to run the installer script with elevated privileges to ensure it's installed as a service. However, I'll also provide full documentation in case manual execution is needed.\n\nBefore installing, I should verify that NSSM is present. Let's check the common installation path:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_2a4fb720529e43078d773be3","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"if (Test-Path 'C:\\Program Files\\nssm\\nssm.exe') { Write-Output 'NSSM found' } else { Write-Output 'NSSM not found' }\"","timeout":10,"elevated":true}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"stepfun/step-3.5-flash:free","usage":{"input":40979,"output":4346,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":45325,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1771419430197}}
